"""Build reviewable milestone reports and email-sized attachment bundles."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,hashlib,zipfile,html,argparse
import pandas as pd
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;OUT=HERE/'outputs/state_trajectory_atlas_v1'
STYLE='body{max-width:1180px;margin:30px auto;padding:0 24px;font:16px/1.55 system-ui;color:#243448}img{width:100%}h2{margin-top:36px}td,th{padding:7px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px}code{overflow-wrap:anywhere}a{color:#087492}'

def entry(f,mime):return dict(path=str(f.resolve()),filename=f.name,mime_type=mime,bytes=f.stat().st_size,sha256=hashlib.sha256(f.read_bytes()).hexdigest())

def milestone1():
 sample=json.loads((OUT/'sampling_manifest.json').read_text());metrics=pd.read_csv(OUT/'pca_preservation.csv');primary=metrics.query('panel=="primary_21" and split=="test" and components in [2,3]')
 def value(transform,estimator,k,col):return float(primary[(primary['transform']==transform)&(primary.estimator==estimator)&(primary.components==k)].iloc[0][col])
 two=value('log1p','observed',2,'temporal_energy_fraction');three=value('log1p','observed',3,'temporal_energy_fraction');median3=value('log1p','observed',3,'median_per_step_energy_fraction')
 body=f'''<h1>Milestone 1: a shared peak-state PCA atlas</h1><p>Each point is one genomic peak at one stage. The same peak is connected across 10, 12, 14, 16, 19 and 24 hpf. The coordinate system is learned once from observed accessibility; fitted trajectories are overlaid through that frozen basis.</p><h2>What is complete</h2><ul><li>20,000 randomly selected training peaks; 5,000 separate held-out peaks; {sample['n_marker_only_peaks']} extra marker-associated peaks for query/display. Whole peak trajectories stay in one split.</li><li>Primary analysis: 21 celltypes present at all six stages. Additional PCA panels compare the same 21 celltypes over 12–24 hpf with 24 celltypes over that window, including optic cup.</li><li>Observed log1p(CPM) and linear-CPM sensitivity bases, retaining every PC. Count/fit support is metadata; low-accessibility features are not removed merely for lacking a supported change.</li><li>Observed and fitted states, linear CPM/hpf rates, mean CPM, and specificity tau are saved separately. Existing trajectories and UMAP coordinates remain unchanged.</li></ul><h2>What the first results say</h2><p>For held-out observed states, the first two log-CPM PCs retain <b>{two:.1%}</b> of pooled temporal-displacement energy; three PCs retain <b>{three:.1%}</b>. The median individual step retains <b>{median3:.1%}</b> in three PCs. Although three PCs explain about 86.8% of observed training-state variance, they retain much less temporal change. These numbers answer different questions: pooled energy gives more weight to large changes. All 21 PCs preserve the entire transformed-space displacement.</p><p>The representative paths make same-peak state changes directly inspectable. They are selected examples, not prevalence estimates. A smooth fitted path is retrospective and can reflect the limited curve model; it is not a prediction or a validated biological lineage.</p><p>Synthetic controls separate uniform opening/closing from increasing specificity. Exact stable states remain stationary. Count-sampling noise can nevertheless produce apparent movement around a stable underlying state. Synthetic UMAP checks also show that display lengths are distorted, even when the true trajectories are known.</p><h2>Preservation diagnostics</h2>{primary[['transform','estimator','components','temporal_energy_fraction','median_per_step_energy_fraction','p10_per_step_energy_fraction']].to_html(index=False,float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}')}<h2>How to interpret these plots</h2><p>Arrowheads connect chronological snapshots of the same peak in a common state space. The five elapsed intervals are 2, 2, 2, 3 and 5 hours. Quantitative rate units remain CPM/hpf; a two-dimensional arrow is not a physical speed. PCA axes need not capture all dynamic directions. Fitted states with low support remain inspectable but do not become supported biological changes merely by being smooth.</p><p>CPM is relative accessibility. Tau measures distribution across the fixed celltype panel and remains depth-sensitive; it does not replace accessibility. Nearby-gene annotations do not establish enhancer targets. Celltype pseudobulks describe population summaries, not tracked individual cells.</p><h2>Next milestone</h2><p>Joint 2D/3D UMAPs use all retained PCA dimensions, with shared observed-state training, exact duplicate-state caching, held-out checks and an offline interactive viewer. Their geometry is assessed without imposing a developmental root or temporal path.</p><h2>Figures</h2>'''
 images=sorted([p for p in OUT.glob('*.png') if p.name.startswith(('00_','00b_','01_shared_pca','02_representative_pca','03_representative_pca','04_accessibility','05_pca_change'))])
 for p in images:body+=f'<h3>{html.escape(p.stem)}</h3><a href="{p.name}"><img src="{p.name}"></a>'
 body+='<h2>Saved files</h2><p>sample_data.h5 contains aligned observed/fitted states and support metadata. state_pca.h5 contains all panel/transform coordinates and basis parameters. Large arrays remain in the shared workspace.</p><p><code>'+str(OUT)+'</code></p>'
 report=OUT/'milestone1_pca_report.html';report.write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Shared state atlas: PCA milestone</title><style>'+STYLE+'</style>'+body)
 mail=f'''<p>Hello,</p><p><b>Milestone 1 is complete: we now have a shared peak-state PCA atlas and actual same-peak trajectories through the six stages.</b></p><p>The basis uses observed accessibility from 20,000 training peaks and is checked on 5,000 separate peaks. Fitted curves are transformed through that same basis. Additional PCA panels support the later optic-cup comparison.</p><ul><li><b>Projection fidelity:</b> two log-CPM PCs retain {two:.1%} of pooled observed temporal-change energy; three retain {three:.1%}. The median individual step retains {median3:.1%} in three PCs. The complete 21-PC representation preserves all transformed-space change.</li><li><b>What is promising:</b> we can follow an identified peak through its accessibility states, and inspect opening/closing separately from changes in specificity.</li><li><b>What needs care:</b> selected smooth fitted paths are retrospective. Synthetic stable programs acquire apparent motion under count noise, and low-dimensional views lose or distort some changes. The figures include those controls.</li></ul><p>The attached PNGs and ZIP include the illustrated report, 2D/3D PCA examples, accessibility-versus-specificity plots, synthetic controls, numerical diagnostics, validation records and code. Full arrays are saved at:<br><code>{OUT}</code></p><p>I am continuing with the joint UMAPs and interactive viewer, followed by the matched-window optic-cup extension. I will email those milestones separately.</p><p>Best,<br>Codex</p>'''
 package('milestone1',report,images,mail,include_patterns=['sampling_manifest.json','pca_*.csv','representative_peaks.csv','synthetic*.json','synthetic*.csv','validation_pca.json','validation_sample_alignment.json'],direct_names=['02_representative_pca_trajectories.png','03_representative_pca_3d.png','05_pca_change_preservation.png'])


def package(name,report,images,mail,include_patterns,direct_names,viewer=None):
 p=OUT/'email_packages'/name;p.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
 tables=[]
 for pattern in include_patterns:tables+=list(OUT.glob(pattern))
 files=sorted(set([report,OUT/'reproduce_state_atlas.txt',OUT/'panel_eligibility.json']+images+[im.with_suffix('.pdf') for im in images if im.with_suffix('.pdf').exists()]+tables))
 archive=p/(name+'_review.zip')
 with zipfile.ZipFile(archive,'w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED,compresslevel=8) as z:
  for f in files:z.write(f,str(f.relative_to(OUT)))
  for pattern in ['state_atlas_*.py','state_atlas_viewer_template.html','verify_state_viewer*','check_state_viewer.cjs']:
   for f in HERE.glob(pattern):
    if f.is_file():z.write(f,'source/'+f.name)
  z.writestr('START_HERE.txt','Open '+report.name+' for the illustrated report.\nLarge scientific arrays remain in the shared workspace:\n'+str(OUT)+'\nSource scripts use existing local inputs and repository helpers.\n')
 with zipfile.ZipFile(archive) as z:assert z.testzip() is None
 attachments=[entry(OUT/n,'image/png') for n in direct_names]+[entry(archive,'application/zip')]
 if viewer:attachments.append(entry(viewer,'application/zip'))
 manifest=dict(attachments=attachments,binary_bytes=sum(x['bytes'] for x in attachments));assert manifest['binary_bytes']<18_000_000,manifest['binary_bytes']
 (p/'delivery_manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2)+'\n');(p/'email_body.html').write_text(mail)
 print(json.dumps(dict(milestone=name,files=len(attachments),binary_bytes=manifest['binary_bytes'])),flush=True)


def figure_html(images):
 return ''.join(f'<h3>{html.escape(p.stem)}</h3><a href="{p.name}"><img src="{p.name}"></a>' for p in images)

def metric_table(panels):
 rows=[]
 for panel in panels:
  for dim in [2,3]:
   d=json.loads((OUT/f'umap_coordinates/{panel}_{dim}d.json').read_text())
   for estimator in ['observed','fitted']:
    m=d[estimator+'_diagnostics'];rows.append(dict(panel=panel,dimensions=dim,estimator=estimator,step_rank_correlation=m['temporal_step_length_spearman'],rate_rank_correlation=m['temporal_rate_magnitude_spearman'],neighbor_recall=m['neighborhood_recall_mean'],trustworthiness=m['trustworthiness'],stationary_with_false_motion=m['n_stationary_intervals_with_false_movement']))
 return pd.DataFrame(rows)

def table(df):return df.to_html(index=False,float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}')

def write_report(filename,title,body):
 report=OUT/filename;report.write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+title+'</title><style>'+STYLE+'</style><h1>'+title+'</h1>'+body);return report

def milestone2():
 metrics=metric_table(['primary_21']);pilot=pd.read_csv(OUT/'umap_pilot_diagnostics.csv');support=pd.read_csv(OUT/'support_stratified_umap.csv')
 images=[OUT/n for n in ['11_selected_state_trajectories_2d.png','11_selected_state_trajectories_3d.png','12_joint_state_umap_stage_maps.png','04_umap_parameter_sensitivity.png','06_change_focused_linear_axes.png','08_support_stratified_umap.png']]
 assert all(x.exists() for x in images)
 m=metrics.query('dimensions==2').set_index('estimator');obs=m.loc['observed','step_rank_correlation'];fit=m.loc['fitted','step_rank_correlation']
 sup=support.query('dimensions==2 and estimator=="fitted" and stratum=="At least 3 supported celltypes"').iloc[0]
 body=f'''<p>Each point is a peak at one stage, represented by accessibility across 21 fixed celltypes. Matching genomic peaks are connected through time. This shared state atlas is separate from the existing fixed peak UMAP.</p>
<h2>Input eligibility</h2><p>The source has 190 of 192 celltype-stage combinations. Only hemangioblasts at 10 hpf and notochord at 24 hpf are absent. The fixed panels additionally require at least 20 cells per pseudobulk at every included stage. This yields 21 celltypes across six stages and 24 across five. Optic cup at 10 hpf exists but fails this cell-count threshold; it is not interpreted as zero accessibility. Panel eligibility is separate from per-peak rate support.</p><h2>Completed</h2><ul><li>Joint 2D/3D UMAPs fitted to observed log1p(CPM) states from 20,000 random training peaks, using all 21 PCs. Held-out peak trajectories and fitted states use the same frozen models.</li><li>Defaults: k=30, min_dist=0.3, Euclidean distance, 200 epochs, fixed seed. A smaller pilot tests k/min_dist combinations, another seed, and linear CPM. Defaults were fixed before viewing results.</li><li>Exact repeated state vectors share coordinates across stages and estimators, preventing artificial movement. Repeated training vectors are embedded once, so they do not receive extra density weight.</li><li>Offline HTML: observed/fitted paths, 2D/3D PCA/UMAP, peak/gene search, celltype curves, support, mean CPM, specificity tau, and full-vector CPM/hpf rates.</li></ul>
<h2>Results and interpretation</h2><p>Actual same-peak changes are inspectable without imposing a root or outward arrows. In primary 2D, held-out step-length rank agreement with full log-CPM space is <b>{obs:.3f} observed</b> and <b>{fit:.3f} fitted</b>. UMAP lengths remain distorted; full celltype vectors provide quantitative change measures.</p><p>For fitted intervals supported in at least three celltypes, correlation rises to <b>{sup.step_length_spearman:.3f}</b> ({int(sup.n_intervals):,} of 3,750 intervals). This is a diagnostic stratum, not a default filter: requiring three changing celltypes would discard genuine celltype-specific patterns. Support is conditional on the count-noise model, not biological replication. All strata appear below.</p><p>The pilot's second seed gives similar broad geometry (pairwise-distance rank agreement about 0.983), while temporal-step fidelity stays modest. Reproducible shape alone is insufficient evidence of accurate velocity. Linear-CPM temporal fidelity is poorer; log1p(CPM) remains the display default. The small sweep tests sensitivity and does not optimize an attractive flow.</p><p>Linear axes learned specifically from training-peak temporal differences retain about 33.7% of held-out observed displacement energy in 3D, versus 31.6% for ordinary PCA. This limited improvement does not give a simple three-dimensional representation of every measured change. Noise and multiple celltype directions can both contribute.</p>
<h2>Primary UMAP diagnostics</h2>{table(metrics)}<p>Temporal diagnostics use 750 held-out peaks. Neighborhood metrics use 1,500 unique held-out states, with neighbors recomputed within that subset. Correlations assess length ordering, not calibrated speed or biological direction.</p>
<h2>Support-stratified diagnostics</h2>{table(support[['estimator','stratum','dimensions','n_intervals','step_length_spearman','rate_length_spearman']])}
<h2>Pilot sensitivity</h2>{table(pilot[['setting','feature_transform','n_neighbors','min_dist','temporal_step_length_spearman','neighborhood_recall_mean','distance_rank_agreement_with_baseline_layout']])}
<h2>Scope</h2><p>The stage maps show a conspicuous redistribution at 24 hpf. This describes observed relative-accessibility profiles; the display alone cannot separate biological development from depth, composition, or other stage-specific measurement effects.</p><p>This is a locus-resolved retrospective state atlas. It does not establish a universal root-to-branch stream. Segments connect measured snapshots; the intervening continuous path is unknown. Whole-course fits are retrospective. Changing subtype composition within celltype pseudobulks can contribute to motion; these are not tracked cells.</p><p>Signed mean CPM/hpf can cancel opposite celltype changes, so full-vector RMS rates and individual curves are also shown. CPM measures relative accessibility and cannot establish absolute global opening. Cis co-accessibility is not used to define this state geometry; the previous atlas root does not orient these arrows.</p>
<h2>Figures</h2>{figure_html(images)}<h2>Files</h2><p>Scientific arrays: sample_data.h5, state_pca.h5, state_umap.h5. Trained models/caches: umap_models/. Portable reports, PNG/PDF, CSV diagnostics, checks, and code accompany this milestone; HTML is zipped separately.</p><p><code>{OUT}</code></p>'''
 report=write_report('milestone2_umap_report.html','Milestone 2: shared peak-state UMAP trajectories',body)
 mail=f'''<p>Hello,</p><p><b>Milestone 2 is complete: joint 2D/3D peak-state UMAPs connect the same peak through developmental time.</b> Each state is its accessibility vector across 21 celltypes with at least 20 cells per pseudobulk at every stage. Observed states define the maps; fitted trajectories use that frozen geometry, with no imposed root direction.</p><ul><li><b>Promising:</b> individual opening/closing and specificity trajectories can be inspected beside their celltype curves.</li><li><b>Limit:</b> 2D step-length agreement with full-space changes is {obs:.2f} observed and {fit:.2f} fitted. Fitted agreement reaches {sup.step_length_spearman:.2f} for intervals supported across at least three celltypes. This conditional-support subset is a diagnostic, not a filter excluding celltype-specific programs.</li><li><b>Sensitivity:</b> a small parameter/seed/normalization comparison supports log-CPM for display, but does not establish globally faithful velocities. The original default parameters were retained.</li></ul><p>The attached PNGs and ZIP contain trajectories, diagnostics, an illustrated report, CSVs, and source. The offline interactive HTML package follows in the same thread.</p><p>My interpretation: <b>useful for selected, supported locus trajectories; not yet evidence for a universal root-to-branch developmental stream.</b> Quantitative opening/closing rates remain in full celltype space, in relative CPM/hpf.</p><p>Workspace: <code>{OUT}</code></p><p>Best,<br>Codex</p>'''
 package('milestone2',report,images,mail,['umap_coordinates/primary_21_*.json','umap_pilot_*.csv','umap_pilot_summary.json','support_*.csv','support_strata_definition.json','change_basis*.csv','change_basis_validation.json','validation_umap*.json','validation_umap*.csv','representative_peaks.csv','software_versions.json','state_viewer_manifest.json','browser_validation/*json'],['11_selected_state_trajectories_2d.png','11_selected_state_trajectories_3d.png','08_support_stratified_umap.png'])

def viewer_package():
 p=OUT/'email_packages'/'interactive';p.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);archive=p/'state_trajectory_explorer.zip'
 with zipfile.ZipFile(archive,'w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED,compresslevel=8) as z:
  for n in ['state_trajectory_explorer.html','state_viewer_readme.txt','state_viewer_manifest.json']:z.write(OUT/n,n)
 with zipfile.ZipFile(archive) as z:assert z.testzip() is None
 files=[entry(archive,'application/zip')];assert files[0]['bytes']<18_000_000
 (p/'delivery_manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(attachments=files,binary_bytes=files[0]['bytes']),indent=2)+'\n')
 (p/'email_body.html').write_text('''<p>Hello,</p><p>Here is the <b>offline interactive peak-state atlas</b> accompanying the milestones. Unzip the attachment and open <code>state_trajectory_explorer.html</code> in a browser; it includes its plotting library and requires no server.</p><p>Start with the example buttons, switch observed/fitted states, 2D/3D PCA or UMAP, and primary or later-stage celltype panels. Search by peak ID or nearby gene and inspect individual celltype curves. The 24-celltype later-stage panel includes optic cup.</p><p>Each moving point is the <b>same genomic peak at successive stages</b>. Full-vector CPM/hpf rates and support appear separately from embedding distances. A searchable subset keeps the HTML portable; the complete sampled atlas remains in the workspace.</p><p>Best,<br>Codex</p>''')
 print(json.dumps(dict(milestone='interactive',binary_bytes=files[0]['bytes'])))

def milestone3():
 metrics=metric_table(['primary_21','matched_21','optic_24']);images=[OUT/n for n in ['06_umap_preservation_diagnostics.png','13_optic_cup_state_trajectories.png']];assert all(x.exists() for x in images)
 body=f'''<p>The later-stage extension is complete, with shared 2D/3D peak-state maps and matched controls. Panels use the same training and held-out peak identities.</p><h2>Three comparisons</h2><table><tr><th>Panel</th><th>Stages</th><th>Features</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>primary_21</td><td>10–24 hpf (six)</td><td>21 celltypes</td><td>Primary view</td></tr><tr><td>matched_21</td><td>12–24 hpf (five)</td><td>The same 21 celltypes</td><td>Change stage window</td></tr><tr><td>optic_24</td><td>12–24 hpf (five)</td><td>21 + optic_cup, fast_muscle, hemangioblasts</td><td>Add three celltypes</td></tr></table><p>Combinations absent or below the inherited 20-cell threshold stay masked. Optic cup at 10 hpf is present but below that threshold; hemangioblasts at 10 hpf is absent. Each panel has its own frozen observed-state basis; maps cannot be overlaid as though their axes were shared. Differences between the five-stage panels cannot be attributed solely to optic cup because three features were added.</p><h2>Display fidelity</h2>{table(metrics)}<p>Diagnostics use identical held-out peak identities and panel-specific state vectors. Correlations quantify how display lengths rank full-space changes, not forecasting skill or absolute accessibility.</p><h2>Matched-control comparisons</h2><p>In 3D, the primary atlas restricted to late stages versus the matched five-stage model has pairwise-distance rank agreement 0.994 and neighborhood overlap 0.740. Adding the three celltypes gives corresponding values 0.970 and 0.410. Broad geometry is similar while local neighborhoods change appreciably. The full panel comparison CSV and JSON are included.</p><h2>Optic-cup examples</h2><p>The figure pairs same-peak state paths with observed/fitted optic-cup curves. Selection uses curve behavior and existing support, not UMAP shape; the selection CSV records criteria. Any celltype feature can contribute to a state path, so its direction need not agree with optic cup alone.</p>{figure_html(images)}<h2>Overall interpretation</h2><p><b>This prototype supports examining individual regulatory-state trajectories.</b> It separates opening/closing from changing specificity. Smoothing and supported-change strata improve interpretability, but low-dimensional geometry still loses or distorts temporal changes. These results do not establish a universal root-to-branch flow; genomic peaks retain their identity and do not physically move.</p><p>A useful next biological analysis would group reproducible temporal patterns in original celltype space, then summarize their paths and opening/closing rates with uncertainty. Broad accessibility can remain an independently measured annotation; it should not determine arrow direction. Depth sensitivity, changing cell composition, enhancer annotation, and replication remain separate from visual coherence.</p><h2>Saved deliverables</h2><p>PNG/PDF, reports, offline interactive HTML with all three panels, aligned input subset, all PCA/UMAP coordinates, models/caches, sensitivity/support tables, independent checks, browser/export audits, source, manifests and email receipts.</p><p><code>{OUT}</code></p>'''
 report=write_report('milestone3_optic_report.html','Milestone 3: later-stage and optic-cup extension',body)
 mail=f'''<p>Hello,</p><p><b>Milestone 3 is complete: the later-stage/optic-cup extension and matched comparisons are finished.</b></p><p>All three atlases are available: 21 celltypes across six stages, the same 21 across 12–24 hpf, and 24 across 12–24 hpf adding optic cup, fast muscle, and hemangioblasts. Absent or below-20-cell combinations stay masked; optic cup at 10 hpf is below the threshold, rather than biologically absent.</p><p>The attached figures show cross-panel fidelity and selected optic-cup opening/closing/transient examples with their underlying curves. The report and CSVs explain selection and validation. The interactive HTML in this thread includes all three panels.</p><p><b>My interpretation:</b> promising for inspecting a regulatory element's accessibility history and separating it from specificity. These results do not yet establish one universal root-to-branch stream. Supported fitted trajectories are more interpretable, but biological rates should come from full celltype vectors.</p><p>All outputs, models, coordinates, reports and source: <code>{OUT}</code></p><p>Best,<br>Codex</p>'''
 package('milestone3',report,images,mail,['umap_full_diagnostics.json','umap_panel_comparison.*','umap_coordinates/*.json','validation_umap*.json','validation_umap*.csv','*optic*selection*.csv','*optic*selection*.json','state_viewer_manifest.json','browser_validation/*json','software_versions.json'],['06_umap_preservation_diagnostics.png','13_optic_cup_state_trajectories.png'])
 index='<p>Each chronological path follows one genomic locus through its celltype accessibility profiles.</p><ul>'+''.join(f'<li><a href="{f}">{name}</a></li>' for f,name in [('milestone1_pca_report.html','Milestone 1: PCA and synthetic controls'),('milestone2_umap_report.html','Milestone 2: joint UMAP and sensitivity'),('milestone3_optic_report.html','Milestone 3: optic-cup extension'),('state_trajectory_explorer.html','Offline interactive atlas')])+'</ul><p>Full arrays and models are stored beside these reports. Email packages contain portable figures, reports, and audit records.</p>'
 write_report('index.html','Shared peak-state atlas',index)

if __name__=='__main__':
 p=argparse.ArgumentParser();p.add_argument('--milestone',choices=['1','2','3','interactive'],default='1');a=p.parse_args();{'1':milestone1,'2':milestone2,'3':milestone3,'interactive':viewer_package}[a.milestone]()
